From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: emuTOS-MCD <darekadumon@gmail.com>
Date: Tue, 11 Aug 2026 02:49:42 +0000
Subject: [PATCH 026/190] segacd: declare 16 sectors per track, and rate-limit
 CDD commands

Two hardware failures, both invisible in emulation.

FORMAT.PRG did not work on a real console. It used logical sector I/O,
which needs the volume to be loggable -- and the only volume anyone
formats is one that is not. On a corrupt disk Getbpb fails, the
media-change flag never clears, and every logical access answers E_CHNG
for ever, so the tool refused to run on exactly the disk it existed to
repair. My headless test formatted a healthy volume, which is the one
case that was never in question; it now corrupts B: first.

Physical mode then exposed an older bug underneath. flop_add_drive()
defaults a drive to a 3.5" floppy's 9 sectors per track and getbpb only
corrects that once it has read a boot sector, while our driver assumes
16. With no valid BPB, floppy_rw split requests on a boundary the
driver does not use and every sector landed elsewhere -- reads well
inside the volume returned ESECNF. Sega CD drives now declare their
real geometry at detection.

CDD: polling alone checksummed clean on hardware, but a command every
VBL gave CD N. The drive publishes status every 1/75 s against our
60 Hz, so commands eventually land mid-exchange; no emulator models it.
Commands are now sent twice a second, and the validity boolean is a
score -- how many of the last sixteen packets checksummed -- because a
boolean cannot tell a dead link from a flaky one.
---
 bios/bios.c   | 12 ++++++++++++
 bios/floppy.c | 11 +++++++++++
 bios/segacd.c | 28 +++++++++++++++++++++++++---
 3 files changed, 48 insertions(+), 3 deletions(-)

diff --git a/bios/bios.c b/bios/bios.c
index b2757a0..d0ae39a 100644
--- a/bios/bios.c
+++ b/bios/bios.c
@@ -885,6 +885,18 @@ void biosmain(void)
         static char buf[64];
         LONG rc;
 
+        {   /* Wreck B: first. The tool's whole job is to recover a
+             * volume that cannot be mounted, so testing it on a healthy
+             * one tests the wrong thing -- which is exactly how the
+             * first version shipped broken. */
+            static char junk[512];
+            int j;
+            for (j = 0; j < 512; j++)
+                junk[j] = (char)(j ^ 0x3C);
+            Rwabs(1 | 8 | 2, (LONG)junk, 1, 0, 1, 0L);
+            cprintf("\033Ecorrupted B: boot sector\r\n");
+        }
+
         call_ikbdraw(0x15);             /* 'Y' press */
         call_ikbdraw(0x95);             /* and release */
         call_ikbdraw(0x39);             /* space, for "press any key" */
diff --git a/bios/floppy.c b/bios/floppy.c
index 8be7f5e..23d0be8 100644
--- a/bios/floppy.c
+++ b/bios/floppy.c
@@ -418,6 +418,17 @@ static void flop_detect_drive(WORD dev)
 #ifdef MACHINE_SEGACD
     if (segacd_flop_detect_drive(dev)) {
         flop_add_drive(dev);
+        /*
+         * Our volumes are 16 sectors per track, one side. The generic
+         * default is a 3.5" floppy's 9, and getbpb only corrects it
+         * once it has successfully read a boot sector -- so on a volume
+         * too damaged to log in, floppy_rw splits requests on a
+         * boundary the driver does not use and every sector lands
+         * somewhere else. Which is precisely when a format utility
+         * needs raw access to work.
+         */
+        blkdev[dev].geometry.spt = 16;
+        blkdev[dev].geometry.sides = 1;
         units[dev].last_access = hz_200;
     }
     return;
diff --git a/bios/segacd.c b/bios/segacd.c
index 5ec9490..f10d86f 100644
--- a/bios/segacd.c
+++ b/bios/segacd.c
@@ -103,7 +103,10 @@ const UBYTE *segacd_physbase(void)
 #define CDD_INFO_ABSMSF 0x00
 
 static UBYTE cdd_stat[10];
-static UBYTE cdd_valid;
+static UBYTE cdd_valid;         /* last packet checksummed */
+static UBYTE cdd_score;         /* valid packets in the last 16, latched */
+static UBYTE cdd_count, cdd_good;
+static UBYTE cdd_tick;
 
 void segacd_cdd_init(void)
 {
@@ -334,11 +337,30 @@ void segacd_int_2_c(void)
         }
     }
 
-    cdd_ask_position();
+    /* The drive publishes status every 1/75 s and we run at 60 Hz, so
+     * writing a command every frame lands in the middle of an exchange
+     * sooner or later. Real hardware notices; the emulators do not.
+     * Ask a couple of times a second instead. */
+    if (++cdd_tick >= 32) {
+        cdd_tick = 0;
+        cdd_ask_position();
+    }
     cdd_poll();
+
+    /* A boolean cannot tell a dead link from a flaky one. Count how
+     * many of the last sixteen packets checksummed and report that:
+     * F is a clean link, 0 is nothing, anything between is a race. */
+    if (cdd_valid)
+        cdd_good++;
+    if (++cdd_count >= 16) {
+        /* sixteen does not fit in the nibble we report it in */
+        cdd_score = (UBYTE)((cdd_good > 15) ? 15 : cdd_good);
+        cdd_count = 0;
+        cdd_good = 0;
+    }
     /* status nibble and the minutes in one word, seconds and frames in
      * the other: no comm register is free enough to carry all seven */
-    *(volatile UWORD *)0xFF8022 = (UWORD)((cdd_valid ? 0x8000 : 0)
+    *(volatile UWORD *)0xFF8022 = (UWORD)(((UWORD)cdd_score << 12)
         | ((UWORD)cdd_stat[0] << 8)
         | ((UWORD)cdd_stat[2] << 4)
         |  (UWORD)cdd_stat[3]);
